# What actually links a hospital contact to a prescription?
prompt: "Why are a pid and a date all you have — and what does that mean for the questions you can ask? Why do you suddenly get three times as many rows after a merge, and what does validate= save you from? Left join or inner join: how do they give two different cohorts from the same patients?"
next: {"title":"Is one E11 code enough to call someone diabetic?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/registerdata-i-praksis-python-og-pandas/holder-en-e11-kode-for-a-kalle-noen.cast"}
enroll: https://drawcast.anvil.app

## One patient, two registers, one key
lang: en
voice: male

    text tittel "One patient, two registers, one key" x 500 y 705 font_size 34 hidden true
    path boks_npr closed #2f6b8f points [[60,380],[470,380],[470,645],[60,645]] hidden true
    text hdr_npr "npr — one row per contact" #2f6b8f x 265 y 608 font_size 24 hidden true
    text npr_pid "pid: 100234" #8a5fa8 x 265 y 558 font_size 25 hidden true
    text npr_dato "dato_inn: 2019-03-14" #2f6b8f x 265 y 513 font_size 25 hidden true
    text npr_ut "dato_ut: 2019-03-18" #2f6b8f x 265 y 468 font_size 25 hidden true
    text npr_icd "icd10: E11.9" #2f6b8f x 265 y 423 font_size 25 hidden true
    path boks_lmr closed #b5482e points [[530,380],[940,380],[940,645],[530,645]] hidden true
    text hdr_lmr "lmr — one row per dispensing" #b5482e x 735 y 608 font_size 24 hidden true
    text lmr_pid "pid: 100234" #8a5fa8 x 735 y 558 font_size 25 hidden true
    text lmr_dato "dato_utlev: 2019-04-02" #b5482e x 735 y 513 font_size 25 hidden true
    text lmr_atc "atc: A10BA02 (metformin)" #b5482e x 735 y 468 font_size 23 hidden true
    text lmr_ddd "ddd: 90" #b5482e x 735 y 423 font_size 25 hidden true
    edge kobling npr_pid -> lmr_pid curved #8a5fa8 hidden true
    text lbl_nokkel "pid = the only link" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 664 font_size 22 hidden true
    path tidslinje #8f887c points [[110,225],[890,225]] hidden true
    shape m1 #2f6b8f shape circle x 200 y 225 radius 9 hidden true
    shape m2 #2f6b8f shape circle x 340 y 225 radius 9 hidden true
    shape m3 #b5482e shape circle x 680 y 225 radius 9 hidden true
    label lbl_m1 "dato_inn 14 Mar" below #2f6b8f attach_to m1 hidden true
    label lbl_m2 "dato_ut 18 Mar" below #2f6b8f attach_to m2 hidden true
    label lbl_m3 "dato_utlev 2 Apr" below #b5482e attach_to m3 hidden true

This lecture is about one thing: what actually links a hospital contact to a prescription — and what doesn't.
    draw tittel

Here is one row from npr, NPR, the Norwegian Patient Registry: patient 100234, admitted on the fourteenth of March 2019, discharged four days later, with main diagnosis E11.9 — type 2 diabetes.
    draw boks_npr hdr_npr npr_pid npr_dato npr_ut npr_icd

    pause 0.4

And here is one row from LMR, Legemiddelregisteret — the Norwegian drug register: on the second of April, metformin is collected at a pharmacy, A10BA02, ninety defined daily doses.
    draw boks_lmr hdr_lmr lmr_pid lmr_dato lmr_atc lmr_ddd

    pause 0.4

The diagnosis sits in one row, the drug in the other. But what inside these rows tells you they belong to the same person?
    focus npr_icd lmr_atc

    pause 1.8

The answer is at the top of both — and it is not a name, a national ID number or a GP.
    camera center.x 500 center.y 555 zoom 1.7

pid: a pseudonymous ID number that the data provider put in place of the national ID number, the same in all three files.
    draw kobling lbl_nokkel

There is no contact ID in the dispensing, and nothing in the contact points back to the prescription. This one column is the whole bridge.
    highlight npr_pid lmr_pid effect glow

And the ID number only lives in your project — in the next data delivery of the same patients, they will have completely different numbers.
    camera reset true

But pid only tells you who. If you want to know whether this prescription belongs to this hospital stay, what else do you need?

    pause 1.8

The dates — the second and last piece of information you have. The stay runs from the fourteenth to the eighteenth of March.
    draw tidslinje m1 m2 lbl_m1 lbl_m2

The dispensing lands here, on the second of April.
    draw m3 lbl_m3

Fifteen days after discharge — or nineteen days after admission. Which of them counts as «the same episode»?
    arrow gap_arrow from.x 345 from.y 278 to.x 675 to.y 278
    label lbl_gap "15 days?" above attach_to gap_arrow

    pause 1.5

The registers don't say: no column tells you this pack of metformin was started because of this stay.
    highlight gap_arrow effect pulse

    clear keep ["tittel"]

So what you have been given is two pieces of information: pid answers who, the dates answer when.
    text sum1 "pid  →  WHO" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 540 font_size 34
    text sum2 "date  →  WHEN" x 500 y 465 font_size 34

Everything else — that the prescription belongs to the contact — is an assumption you have to write into the code yourself.
    text sum3 "The link between them is yours to make." x 500 y 365 font_size 28
    mark mark_sum target sum3 kind box
    delivery grave

In the next part we'll see how a merge on pid alone can quietly multiply your rows — and your drug use.
    text neste "Part 2: why a merge multiplies your rows" #8f887c x 500 y 255 font_size 24

## Why did the rows suddenly multiply?
lang: en
voice: male

    text tittel "Why did the rows suddenly multiply?" x 500 y 700 font_size 34 hidden true
    box k1 "npr: contact 12 Mar" #2f6b8f hidden true
    box k2 "npr: contact 4 Sep" #2f6b8f hidden true
    box u1 "lmr: disp. 20 Feb" #b5482e hidden true
    box u2 "lmr: disp. 18 May" #b5482e hidden true
    box u3 "lmr: disp. 14 Aug" #b5482e hidden true
    label lab_npr "E11, pid 4711" left #2f6b8f attach_to k1 hidden true
    label lab_lmr "A10BA02 — 90 DDD each" right #b5482e attach_to u2 hidden true
    edge e11 k1 -> u1 #8f887c hidden true
    edge e12 k1 -> u2 #8f887c hidden true
    edge e13 k1 -> u3 #8f887c hidden true
    edge e21 k2 -> u1 #8f887c hidden true
    edge e22 k2 -> u2 #8f887c hidden true
    edge e23 k2 -> u3 #8f887c hidden true
    text sum_text "2 contacts × 3 dispensings = 6 rows" x 500 y 110 font_size 30 hidden true
    mark sum_box target sum_text kind box hidden true
    ```python kode x 500 y 400 width 940 font_size 20 show above hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    npr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [4711, 4711],
        "dato_inn": ["2019-03-12", "2019-09-04"]})
    lmr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [4711, 4711, 4711],
        "dato_utlev": ["2019-02-20", "2019-05-18",
                       "2019-08-14"],
        "ddd": [90, 90, 90]})
    m = pd.merge(npr, lmr, on="pid")
    print("rows:", len(npr), len(lmr), "->", len(m))
    print("ddd:", lmr["ddd"].sum(), "->", m["ddd"].sum())
    ```

Now for the merge itself: joining on pid alone can double the drug use in your data without saying a word about it.
    draw tittel

Patient 4711 has two contacts in npr — one in March, one in September — both coded E11.
    draw k1 k2 lab_npr

The same pid has three dispensings in lmr: metformin, A10BA02, ninety DDD on each pack.
    draw u1 u2 u3 lab_lmr

Five rows go in. How many rows come out of pd.merge on pid?

    pause 1.8

Every contact is paired with every dispensing — pandas has no date information to choose with.
    draw e11 e12 e13 e21 e22 e23 parallel true

Two times three: six rows, where we only had five events.
    draw sum_text sum_box

The March contact now sits next to the August dispensing — a pair that matches no clinical event at all.
    point at.ref e13 gesture circle

Let's count the same thing in pandas, with the numbers in front of us.
    clear keep ["tittel"]

npr: two rows for pid 4711, each with its own admission date.
    draw kode kode_line_1 kode_line_2 kode_line_3 parallel true

lmr: three dispensings, ninety DDD each — two hundred and seventy DDD in total.
    draw kode_line_4 kode_line_5 kode_line_6 kode_line_7

What do you think the DDD sum will be after the merge?

    pause 1.8

pd.merge on pid, with no date and no validate — a many-to-many merge that happens in complete silence, so we ask for the row count and the DDD sum.
    draw kode_line_8 kode_line_9 kode_line_10

Six rows — and two hundred and seventy DDD have become five hundred and forty.
    draw kode_out delivery grave

Each dispensing is counted again for every contact the patient had: twice here, because she was admitted twice.
    camera center.ref kode_out zoom 1.8

    camera reset true

And the inflation hits the sickest hardest: ten contacts and twenty prescriptions give two hundred rows for a single person, so exactly those with the most disease weigh most in an analysis they were never meant to dominate.
    clear keep ["tittel"]

Rows aren't patients, they're pairs — and anything you sum after a merge like this counts the same dispensing over and over.
    text konkl "Rows aren't patients — they're pairs" x 500 y 430 font_size 34
    mark konkl_box target konkl kind box
    text konkl2 "summed ddd counts the same dispensing again" #8f887c x 500 y 340 font_size 24

## validate= as a guard against silent errors
lang: en
voice: male

    text tittel "validate= — the guard against silent errors" x 500 y 702 font_size 34 hidden true
    ```python kode x 500 y 415 width 920 font_size 15 show above hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    npr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [1, 1, 2, 3], "icd10": "E11"})
    lmr = pd.DataFrame({"pid": [1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3], "atc": "A10BA02"})
    print("npr:", len(npr), "lmr:", len(lmr))
    merged = npr.merge(lmr, on="pid", how="inner")
    print("after merge:", len(merged))
    try:
        npr.merge(lmr, on="pid", how="inner", validate="one_to_many")
    except pd.errors.MergeError as e:
        print("MergeError:", e)
    kohort = npr.drop_duplicates("pid")
    check = kohort.merge(lmr, on="pid", how="inner", validate="one_to_many")
    print("unique pid:", len(check))
    ```
    box n_kontakt "E11 contact 4 Mar 2019" #2f6b8f x 230 y 520 hidden true
    box n_utlev "A10BA02 dispensed 17 Nov 2023" #b5482e x 770 y 520 hidden true
    arrow arr n_kontakt -> n_utlev curved #8f887c hidden true

We have seen a merge on pid quietly multiply the rows. Now let's make pandas refuse to do that merge at all.
    draw tittel

Four E11 contacts spread over three patients — pid 1 has two stays — and nine A10 dispensings.
    draw kode kode_line_1 kode_line_2 kode_line_3 parallel true

And here is the same kind of merge as in the last part: on pid alone, with no rule about how many rows each side is allowed to have.
    draw kode_line_4 kode_line_5 kode_line_6

Why doesn't pandas complain here, when the result has three times as many rows as npr?

    pause 1.8

Because many-to-many is perfectly legal pandas — it cannot guess what you had in mind.
    highlight kode_line_5 effect glow

So we state the assumption out loud: validate equals one_to_many promises that pid is unique in npr — the short form is 1:m, and its siblings are m:1, 1:1 and m:m, where m:m is the one that checks nothing at all.
    draw kode_line_7 kode_line_8 kode_line_9 kode_line_10

At the bottom we do the same thing, but with one row per patient before the merge.
    draw kode_line_11 kode_line_12 kode_line_13

What does the script print now — twelve rows, or no table at all?

    pause 1.8

Four and nine rows in, twelve rows out of the unguarded merge — and then the guarded one stops.
    draw kode_out

MergeError: Merge keys are not unique in left dataset; not a one-to-many merge.
    camera center.ref kode_out zoom 1.8

With drop_duplicates on pid it goes through: nine rows, one per dispensing, just as promised.
    camera reset true

The strength is where the error appears — in the line that merges, not three steps later in a table that looks perfectly fine.
    point at.ref kode_line_8 gesture underline delivery grave

But does a passing validate mean the merge actually makes clinical sense?
    clear keep ["tittel"]

    pause 1.8

Here is a pair that passes the test with flying colours: an E11 contact in March 2019 and a metformin dispensing in November 2023.
    draw n_kontakt arr n_utlev

Same pid, four years and eight months apart — merge doesn't care, and validate only counts rows.
    label lab_gap "same pid — 4 yrs 8 mo apart" above #8f887c attach_to arr

validate= guards against the wrong cardinality, not against meaningless matches — the date rule is yours to set.
    text t_ok "validate=\"one_to_many\" → no error" x 500 y 380 font_size 26
    text t_konkl "Cardinality ≠ clinical meaning" #b5482e x 500 y 290 font_size 30
    mark boks target t_konkl kind box
    delivery grave

## Left or inner: two cohorts from the same patients
lang: en
voice: male
use: venn_diagram
with:
    sets:
      - {label: 'E11 in npr (4,200)', only_label: 'Code only: 850'}
      - {label: 'A10BA02 in lmr (5,100)', only_label: 'Metformin only: 1,750'}
    overlap_labels:
      ab: 'Both: 3,350'
    shade:
      - a
      - ab
    universe_box: true
    title: Same pid universe, two cohorts

    ```python kode x 500 y 420 width 900 font_size 19 show above hidden true
    import pandas as pd
    e11 = pd.DataFrame({"pid": list(range(1, 4201))})
    met = pd.DataFrame({"pid": list(range(851, 5951))})
    kohort_left = e11.merge(met, on="pid", how="left", validate="1:1")
    kohort_inner = e11.merge(met, on="pid", how="inner", validate="1:1")
    print("left :", len(kohort_left), "patients")
    print("inner:", len(kohort_inner), "patients")
    ```

Now that validate= guards the cardinality, one word remains that quietly decides who your cohort is: left or inner.
    draw title

    draw universe

This circle is 4,200 adults with at least one E11 contact in npr — our diabetes diagnosis.
    draw set_0 set_label_0

And this one is 5,100 people with at least one dispensing of metformin, A10BA02, in lmr.
    draw set_1 set_label_1

How many of the 4,200 do you think also turn up with a metformin dispensing within the 365-day time window?
    highlight set_0 set_1 effect glow

    pause 1.8

3,350 — the overlap is the patients who have both the code in npr and the dispensing in lmr.
    draw shade_ab region_label_ab

That is exactly the set an inner join gives you back.
    point at.ref region_label_ab gesture circle

But who are the 850 E11 patients left outside the overlap?
    point at.ref set_label_0 gesture tap

    pause 1.8

People managed on diet alone, insulin users, and those diagnosed late in the year — while the 1,750 on the other side use metformin without an E11 code in npr, many of them diabetics treated only by their GP, and some for PCOS.
    draw shade_a region_label_a region_label_b

After a left join the 850 are still there, one row each, but with NaN in atc, dato_utlev and ddd.
    camera center.ref shade_a zoom 1.8

    camera reset true

    clear

In pandas the whole difference is one word. Here are the two tables, reduced to one row per pid.
    draw kode kode_line_1 kode_line_2 kode_line_3 parallel true

Same key, same validate — only left swapped for inner.
    draw kode_line_4 kode_line_5

How many rows are left in each?
    draw kode_line_6 kode_line_7

    pause 1.5

4,200 against 3,350. One word, a difference of 850 patients.
    draw kode_out

And the 850 don't vanish at random: an inner join systematically removes those without drug treatment, and makes the cohort sicker than the population you meant to describe.
    highlight kode_out effect glow delivery grave

    clear

The merge rule from this whole lecture: define the time window first, check cardinality with validate, and choose the join type on purpose.
    text regel_head "The merge rule" x 500 y 580 font_size 40
    text r1 "1. Define the time window before you merge" x 500 y 470 font_size 30
    text r2 "2. Check cardinality with validate=" x 500 y 390 font_size 30
    text r3 "3. Choose the join on purpose — it defines the cohort" x 500 y 310 font_size 30

Because pid and a date only give you the right to merge — the time window and the join type decide who your patients really are.
    mark mark target r3 kind box
    delivery grave

